[32m+[m[32m# ADR-008 — Editor Experience is Part of the Product[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Status[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mAccepted[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Decision[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe Block Editor is considered a first-class user interface of the Meybell Framework.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe editing experience shall receive the same level of intentional design, usability, accessibility, and documentation as the public-facing website.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mFeatures, design tokens, and reusable components should be implemented in ways that create consistency between content creation and content presentation.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mWhen a choice exists between making development easier and making content management clearer, the framework should favor the long-term experience of the content editor whenever reasonably practical.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Why[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mA website is not finished when it is launched.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIt becomes a tool that another person must use, often for years.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mMany organizations spend dozens or hundreds of hours maintaining their websites after launch. Editors should not need to mentally translate between "what I see while editing" and "what visitors actually see."[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mA confusing editing experience leads to:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- inconsistent branding[m
[32m+[m[32m- accidental design drift[m
[32m+[m[32m- unnecessary training[m
[32m+[m[32m- slower content updates[m
[32m+[m[32m- reduced confidence[m
[32m+[m[32m- abandoned CMS features[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mConversely, an editor that mirrors the published experience allows content creators to work with confidence and consistency.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe framework therefore considers editor experience to be part of the product—not merely a development convenience.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Documentation Philosophy[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mDocumentation should describe editor-facing decisions alongside developer-facing decisions.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mWhen introducing a new feature or component, documentation should answer two questions:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m> **How does this improve the experience for the visitor?**[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mand[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m> **How does this improve the experience for the person maintaining the website?**[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIf documentation only addresses developers, it is incomplete.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIf a design decision intentionally changes the editor experience, that rationale should be documented.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe goal is not simply to make websites easier to build.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe goal is to make websites easier to own.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Examples[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Good[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- Custom color palettes appear identically within the editor and on the live website.[m
[32m+[m[32m- Typography shown while editing closely resembles the published page.[m
[32m+[m[32m- Buttons inserted through Gutenberg automatically inherit framework spacing, border radius, and typography.[m
[32m+[m[32m- Reusable blocks provide meaningful names and descriptions rather than technical jargon.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mExample:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m```text[m
[32m+[m[32m❌ CTA_Component_01[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m✅ Call to Action[m
[32m+[m[32mEncourage visitors to take the next step.[m
